description:
{panel:bgColor=#e7f4fa}
  *NOTE:* This suggestion is for *JIRA Server*. Using *JIRA Cloud*? [See the corresponding suggestion|http://jira.atlassian.com/browse/JRACLOUD-35617].
  {panel}

Currently the documentaion [Cloning an Issue|https://confluence.atlassian.com/display/JIRA/Cloning+an+Issue] mentions a list of fields whose values would be retained in the clone.

The current behaviour is that when an issue is cloned, it's resolution and status is reset back to the unresolved and the starting status according to the workflow (e.g Open or New etc) respectively.

Some users basically are confused about what is the expected behaviour with regards to the issues status and resolution, when an issue is cloned. No reference to this is made in the documenation currently, it should be mentioned.

{panel:bgColor=#e7f4fa}
  *NOTE:* This suggestion is for *JIRA Server*. Using *JIRA Cloud*? [See the corresponding suggestion|http://jira.atlassian.com/browse/JRACLOUD-37038].
  {panel}


Because the Resolution field behaves in a very specific way which isn't obvious to new Administrators. They can put themselves in a bad state very easily (like by adding the Resolution field to the create screen) but getting out of it isn't as simple (this requires workflow changes and bulk operations).

With this kind of documentation customers can understand the impact of the use of this field and why JIRA behaves in this way.

summary_description_acceptance:
Documentation Request: Clarification regarding Resolution field. $end$ {panel:bgColor=#e7f4fa}
  *NOTE:* This suggestion is for *JIRA Server*. Using *JIRA Cloud*? [See the corresponding suggestion|http://jira.atlassian.com/browse/JRACLOUD-37038].
  {panel}


