description:
{panel:bgColor=#e7f4fa}
  *NOTE:* This suggestion is for *JIRA Server*. Using *JIRA Cloud*? [See the corresponding suggestion|http://jira.atlassian.com/browse/JRACLOUD-60144].
  {panel}

In the restricted functions documentation for Cloud it is never mentioned that you can only use the standard ports when configuring an inbound mail server either in JIRA or in JIRA Service Desk.

This is because our SOCK proxy is configured to only allow below ports:
- IMAP: 143
- IMAPS: 993
- POP3: 110
- POP3S: 995



This is actually mentioned in a comment from George Barnett on [Outbound connectivity: should we keep our current squid+SOCKS layer 7 proxy design, identify an improved layer 7 proxy design, or transition to NAT?|https://extranet.atlassian.com/pages/viewpage.action?pageId=2471665094&focusedCommentId=2471408313#comment-2471408313]

{quote}
On the socks 5 server side of things, set a simple whitelist of allowed ports.  This will mean that in the event of a compromise we won't become a botnet host.......
{quote}

original_description_sprint:
In the restricted functions documentation for Cloud it is never mentioned that you can only use the standard ports when configuring an inbound mail server either in JIRA or in JIRA Service Desk.

This is because our SOCK proxy is configured as below:
{noformat}
# MAIL - IMAP(s)
#
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 143
}
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 993
}

# MAIL - XMPP(s)
#
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 5222
}
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 5269
}

# MAIL - POP
#
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 110
}
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 995
}
{noformat}



The configuration above is actually mentioned in a comment from George Barnett on [Outbound connectivity: should we keep our current squid+SOCKS layer 7 proxy design, identify an improved layer 7 proxy design, or transition to NAT?|https://extranet.atlassian.com/pages/viewpage.action?pageId=2471665094&focusedCommentId=2471408313#comment-2471408313]

{quote}
On the socks 5 server side of things, set a simple whitelist of allowed ports.  This will mean that in the event of a compromise we won't become a botnet host.......
{quote}

original_summary_description_acceptance_:
Restricted Function documentation for Cloud should mention that you can only use standard ports in incoming mail server configuration $end$ In the restricted functions documentation for Cloud it is never mentioned that you can only use the standard ports when configuring an inbound mail server either in JIRA or in JIRA Service Desk.

This is because our SOCK proxy is configured as below:
{noformat}
# MAIL - IMAP(s)
#
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 143
}
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 993
}

# MAIL - XMPP(s)
#
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 5222
}
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 5269
}

# MAIL - POP
#
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 110
}
pass {
   from: 10.0.0.0/8 port 1-65535 to: 0.0.0.0/0 port = 995
}
{noformat}



The configuration above is actually mentioned in a comment from George Barnett on [Outbound connectivity: should we keep our current squid+SOCKS layer 7 proxy design, identify an improved layer 7 proxy design, or transition to NAT?|https://extranet.atlassian.com/pages/viewpage.action?pageId=2471665094&focusedCommentId=2471408313#comment-2471408313]

{quote}
On the socks 5 server side of things, set a simple whitelist of allowed ports.  This will mean that in the event of a compromise we won't become a botnet host.......
{quote}
 $acceptance criteria:$

description:
{panel:bgColor=#e7f4fa}
  *NOTE:* This suggestion is for *JIRA Server*. Using *JIRA Cloud*? [See the corresponding suggestion|http://jira.atlassian.com/browse/JRACLOUD-60539].
  {panel}

The [email handler documentation|https://confluence.atlassian.com/adminjiracloud/creating-issues-and-comments-from-email-779288896.html#Creatingissuesandcommentsfromemail-messagehandlersMailhandlers] for [Create a new issue from each email message|https://confluence.atlassian.com/adminjiracloud/creating-issues-and-comments-from-email-779288896.html#Creatingissuesandcommentsfromemail-Createanewissuefromeachemailmessage] is wrong. Indeed, for the *Project* field, it says:
{quote}
*Project*	
Specify the project key of the default project to which new issues are created by this handler — for example, JRA.
+Note:+
This field is only relevant for issue creation, not for issue commenting.
If an email message contains an issue key in its subject line and that issue key exists in your JIRA installation, the handler will add the email message content as a comment on the issue, regardless of which project the issue is in. 
{quote}

The above text is the same as the one for [Create a new issue or add a comment to an existing issue|https://confluence.atlassian.com/adminjiracloud/creating-issues-and-comments-from-email-779288896.html#Creatingissuesandcommentsfromemail-Createanewissueoraddacommenttoanexistingissue], but then if that was the case there wouldn't be any difference among the 2.

Indeed, if I reply to a notification from ISSUE-A, I have the email handler configured to ["Create a new issue from each email message"|https://confluence.atlassian.com/adminjiracloud/creating-issues-and-comments-from-email-779288896.html#Creatingissuesandcommentsfromemail-Createanewissuefromeachemailmessage] and the *Project* field is set to PROJECT-Y. Then my reply will create a new issue in PROJECT-Y instead of adding a comment to ISSUE-A.

{panel:bgColor=#e7f4fa}
  *NOTE:* This suggestion is for *JIRA Server*. Using *JIRA Cloud*? [See the corresponding suggestion|http://jira.atlassian.com/browse/JRACLOUD-60936].
  {panel}

h3. Summary
Attachments from JIRA in Cloud are now stored in the blobstore w/ Amazon, and may not be included in the customer's disk usage. The [Atlassian Cloud Storage Policy|https://confluence.atlassian.com/cloud/atlassian-cloud-storage-policy-744721619.html] mentions this:

{quote}Disk usage is primarily made up of:
* Attachments from JIRA applications{quote}

If we are indeed not including Amazon cloud storage of attachments, and can the documentation be updated to reflect this?

h3. Environment
* Atlassian Cloud

summary_description_acceptance:
Cloud Storage Documentation still mentions attachments from JIRA accounting for storage $end$ {panel:bgColor=#e7f4fa}
  *NOTE:* This suggestion is for *JIRA Server*. Using *JIRA Cloud*? [See the corresponding suggestion|http://jira.atlassian.com/browse/JRACLOUD-60936].
  {panel}

h3. Summary
Attachments from JIRA in Cloud are now stored in the blobstore w/ Amazon, and may not be included in the customer's disk usage. The [Atlassian Cloud Storage Policy|https://confluence.atlassian.com/cloud/atlassian-cloud-storage-policy-744721619.html] mentions this:

{quote}Disk usage is primarily made up of:
* Attachments from JIRA applications{quote}

If we are indeed not including Amazon cloud storage of attachments, and can the documentation be updated to reflect this?

h3. Environment
* Atlassian Cloud $acceptance criteria:$

